            {
              "end_index": 8830,
              "start_index": 8745,
              "title": "Hakeem Jeffries",
              "type": "url_citation",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakeem_Jeffries?utm_source=openai"
            },
            {
              "end_index": 9225,
              "start_index": 9128,
              "title": "Congressman Hakeem Jeffries - Turning Point Action",
              "type": "url_citation",
              "url": "https://www.tpaction.com/scorecard/legislators/hakeemjeffries?utm_source=openai"
            },
            {
              "end_index": 9588,
              "start_index": 9490,
              "title": "Rep. Hakeem Jeffries - Scorecard 117: 0% | Heritage Action",
              "type": "url_citation",
              "url": "https://heritageaction.com/scorecard/members/J000294/117?utm_source=openai"
            }
          ],
          "text": "1) Answer: Evidence that Hakeem Jeffries values human rights\n\n- Policing and civil-rights reform: Jeffries was an original cosponsor of the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act of 2020 and argued on the House floor for ending racial profiling, banning chokeholds, and creating national standards on use of force. He also authored the Eric Garner Excessive Use of Force Prevention Act to make chokeholds illegal under federal civil-rights law (folded into the broader policing package). These are core human-rights protections against state violence. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/116th-congress/house-bill/7120/all-info?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Protecting women from violence: He was an original cosponsor of the Violence Against Women Act Reauthorization Act of 2021, which the House passed on March 17, 2021 (244–172), strengthening services and legal protections for survivors. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1620/cosponsors?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Combating hate crimes and racial terror: The House passed the Emmett Till Antilynching Act on February 28, 2022 (422–3), making lynching a federal hate crime—legislation Jeffries publicly supported as House Democratic leader when the Emmett Till national monument was designated. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202247?utm_source=openai))\n\n- LGBTQ equality: Jeffries cosponsored the Equality Act (H.R. 5) and participated in Judiciary Committee hearings supporting federal nondiscrimination protections—an explicit commitment to equal treatment in employment, housing, credit, education and public accommodations. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/5/text/ih?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Criminal-justice fairness: He led the bipartisan EQUAL Act to eliminate the crack/powder cocaine sentencing disparity and apply relief retroactively—human-rights–aligned reform addressing discriminatory punishment. The bill passed the House in 2021. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1693/cosponsors?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Fighting human trafficking: Jeffries introduced and helped enact the Human Trafficking Prevention Act, requiring the National Human Trafficking Hotline to be posted in airports, trains, buses and federal buildings; the House approved it unanimously in 2022. ([jeffries.house.gov](https://jeffries.house.gov/2022/09/20/house-passes-reps-jeffries-issa-bass-mace-bill-to-expand-human-traffickingprevention-measures/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- International human rights: He co-introduced the Turkey Human Rights Promotion Act of 2021 (with Rep. Liz Cheney) pressing for rule of law, press freedom, and an end to torture. He also supported measures to keep goods made with forced labor out of U.S. supply chains; the House passed the Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act 428–1 on December 8, 2021. ([jeffries.house.gov](https://jeffries.house.gov/2021/07/22/reps-jeffries-cheney-introduce-bipartisan-bill-to-promote-human-rights-in-turkey/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Independent civil-liberties scoring: In the 117th Congress, the ACLU’s legislative scorecard rated Jeffries at 100% alignment with civil-liberties positions across key votes—an external indicator that his voting behavior consistently advances rights protections. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/ny-hakeem-jeffries/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Consistent public advocacy: Beyond votes, he has led or joined initiatives to protect Jewish communities from antisemitism and has called for humanitarian relief, release of hostages, and a rights-respecting two-state solution amid the Gaza crisis—positions rooted in protection of civilians and equal human dignity. He also advanced the 21st Century Respect Act to remove derogatory racial/ethnic terms from federal law and pressed for better hate-crime reporting. ([jeffries.house.gov](https://jeffries.house.gov/2024/04/29/letter-to-speaker-mike-johnson-on-the-bipartisan-countering-antisemitism-act/?utm_source=openai))\n\nTaken together—sponsoring and passing rights-expanding bills, supporting international accountability, earning top civil-liberties ratings, and repeatedly advocating for vulnerable groups—Jeffries’ record is consistent with valuing and working to advance human rights.\n\n\n2) Sources I considered TRUSTWORTHY\n\n- Source: Congress.gov (Library of Congress)\n  - Reasoning 1: Official, nonpartisan repository of U.S. federal legislation, maintained by the Library of Congress.\n  - Reasoning 2: Primary documentation for Jeffries’ sponsorship/cosponsorship and bill status used above (e.g., EQUAL Act; Equality Act; VAWA; policing bill). ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1693/cosponsors?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives\n  - Reasoning 1: Official vote tallies maintained by House Tally Clerks; authoritative for roll-call outcomes.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used to verify House passage and vote margins (UFLPA 428–1; Emmett Till Antilynching Act 422–3). ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2021412?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Congressional Record via GovInfo (U.S. Government Publishing Office)\n  - Reasoning 1: Official verbatim record of congressional proceedings; primary source.\n  - Reasoning 2: Confirms Jeffries’ participation and supportive statements in Equality Act hearing. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/event/116th-congress/house-event/LC66875/text?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: jeffries.house.gov (official congressional site)\n  - Reasoning 1: Primary source for Jeffries’ own legislative announcements and statements.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used to verify his authorship/leadership on policing reforms, human-trafficking law, Turkey Human Rights Promotion Act, antisemitism initiative, DEI/civil-rights statements, and Respect Act. ([jeffries.house.gov](https://jeffries.house.gov/2021/03/01/rep-jeffries-national-action-network-gwen-carr-announce-reintroduction-of-bill-to-criminalize-the-chokehold/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: ACLU Legislative Scorecard\n  - Reasoning 1: Reputable, long-standing civil-liberties NGO with transparent vote-tracking.\n  - Reasoning 2: Provides an independent quantitative measure of Jeffries’ rights-aligned voting in the 117th Congress (100%). ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/ny-hakeem-jeffries/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: USCIRF (U.S. Commission on International Religious Freedom)\n  - Reasoning 1: U.S. government commission; bipartisan and research-based.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used for context on Global Magnitsky’s human-rights accountability tool (demonstrates the type of sanctions framework Jeffries has supported in related packages). ([uscirf.gov](https://www.uscirf.gov/news-room/releases-statements/uscirf-applauds-permanent-reauthorization-global-magnitsky-human?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Freedom House\n  - Reasoning 1: Widely cited nonpartisan NGO evaluating global freedom/human rights.\n  - Reasoning 2: Referenced for background on congressional efforts to reauthorize Global Magnitsky sanctions as a human-rights tool. ([freedomhouse.org](https://freedomhouse.org/article/freedom-house-human-rights-first-56-organizations-urge-congress-reauthorize-strengthen?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Transparency International U.S.\n  - Reasoning 1: Reputable anticorruption NGO; policy analysis and congressional monitoring.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used for context on inclusion of Magnitsky reauthorization in House packages strengthening human-rights accountability. ([us.transparency.org](https://us.transparency.org/news/house-passes-four-anticorruption-bills-via-america-competes-act/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Axios\n  - Reasoning 1: Recognized mainstream outlet with straightforward Hill coverage; useful for quick context.\n  - Reasoning 2: Cited for summary of House passage of the Emmett Till Act 